                          a pyramid, so grip force does not depend on which way
                          the object happens to slide.
       impratio="100"   - frictional constraints are made 100x stiffer than
                          normal ones. At the default of 1 a contact that is
                          well inside its friction cone still creeps under load,
                          which is what made a firmly gripped cube slide out of
                          the jaws a millimetre at a time. -->
  <option cone="elliptic" impratio="100"/>

  <!-- The gripper is one actuator on finger_l with finger_r tied to it by the
       equality constraint onshape-to-robot emits from the CAD mimic. At the
       default softness that constraint stretches by ~10 mm under grip load, so
       the jaws closed asymmetrically, squeezed the cube off-centre and
       chattered. Raising its impedance makes the two fingers actually track
       each other. The equality element carries no class, so it picks up the
       main default class defined here. -->
  <default>
    <equality solref="0.004 1" solimp="0.995 0.9999 0.0001 0.5 2"/>
  </default>

